// F207: `push` and `insert` became resolver-owned declarations, and each set now dispatches
// **two different ways** -- the shape `add` has had since F199, which is why it needed no new
// mechanism.
//
// The array half stays `builtin fn`, run by its builtin id; the MutableArray half is a `native fn`,
// so the resolver records it on `call_fn` and interpreter.c derives the C symbol from parameter
// one -- `mutable_array_push`, `mutable_array_insert`. That fallback reads parameter one *only*
// (F205 found this when it stopped two `append` declarations from sharing a name), which is
// exactly the discrimination these sets need, parameter one being where they differ. No arm
// rewrites the callee any more.
//
// So this fixture is about dispatch, not about types: if the two halves were ever to be confused,
// a MutableArray `push` would run the array implementation and answer a new array instead of
// writing the container -- which would show up here as the builder not having changed.
fn main() {
    // The immutable half: answers a new array, leaves the original alone.
    val a = [1, 2]
    val once = push(a, 3)
    val many = push(a, 3, 4, 5)
    val placed = insert(a, 0, 0)
    print(a)
    print(once)
    print(many)
    print(placed)

    // The builder half: answers nothing, writes the container. Same two words.
    val b = mutable([1, 2])
    push(b, 3)
    push(b, 4, 5)
    insert(b, 0, 0)
    print(snapshot(b))
    print(len(b))

    // The spread, which is what the conversion actually broke. `push(a, [3, 4])` was refused by
    // the arm and is accepted by the declaration (`values: variadic T`), so deleting the arm woke
    // a path the builtin id had never been given: `collapse_variadic_args` runs only for a
    // `call_fn`, so `call_push` appended the `[int]` itself and printed a garbage element. Both
    // readings of one trailing array are below, and which one applies comes from its static type.
    print(push(a, [3, 4]))

    // A nested element type, where a lone array argument is one element and not a spread -- the
    // ambiguity docs/design/journal/'s `_all` paragraph guards against, on both halves.
    val nested = [[9]]
    print(push(nested, [1, 2]))
    print(push(nested, [[1, 2], [3]]))
    val nb = mutable(nested)
    push(nb, [1, 2])
    print(snapshot(nb))
    push(nb, [[7], [8]])
    print(snapshot(nb))

    // And the spread on the builder half, which never broke -- it goes through
    // `collapse_variadic_args` on the `call_fn` path. Here so the two halves are asserted to agree
    // about one trailing array, which is the property the two dispatch mechanisms could drift on.
    val fb = mutable([1, 2])
    push(fb, [3, 4])
    print(snapshot(fb))
}
